```cpp
void PhaseOneDecompressor::decompressStrip(const PhaseOneStrip& strip) const {
    uint32 width = mRaw->dim.x;
    // ...
    BitPumpMSB32 pump(strip.bs);
    // ...
    auto* img = reinterpret_cast<ushort16*>(mRaw->getData(0, strip.n));
    for (uint32 col = 0; col < width; col++) {
        // ...
        int i = len[col & 1];
        if (i == 14)
            img[col] = pred[col & 1] = pump.getBits(16);
        else {
            pred[col & 1] += static_cast<signed>(pump.getBits(i)) + 1 - (1 << (i - 1));
            img[col] = ushort16(pred[col & 1]);
        }
    }
}
```

The `len` array is initialized to `{0, 0}`. For `col == 0`, the length initialization loop runs. If the first bit read is 1, `j == 0`, and since `col == 0`, it throws `ThrowRDE("Can not initialize lengths. Data is corrupt.")`. This exception is caught by `start_routine`.

But what if the first bit is 0? Then `j` becomes 1, and the loop continues. If the second bit is also 0, `j` becomes 2, etc. If all 5 bits are 0, `j` becomes 5, and the loop exits. Then `j > 0` is true, so `i = length[2 * (5 - 1) + pump.getBits(1)] = length[8 + pump.getBits(1)]`. `length[8] = 14` and `length[9] = 13`. So `i` is either 14 or 13.

If the first bit is 1 (at j=0), then for col==0, it throws. For col>0, it breaks with j=0, and `i` stays at its previous value (which was set in a previous iteration).

So the length initialization is fine. The issue must be elsewhere.

Let me think about what happens when `i = 0`. The `len` array is initialized to `{0, 0}`. For `col == 0`, the length initialization loop runs. If the first bit is 1, it throws. So `len[0]` and `len[1]` can never be 0 after the first column.

But what about `col > 0` when `(col & 7) != 0`? The length initialization only runs when `(col & 7) == 0`. So for columns 1-7, 9-15, etc., the length is not re-initialized, and `len[col & 1]` uses the value from the previous initialization.

So `len[0]` and `len[1]` are always initialized before use. They can't be 0.

